ltsp (6.3.15-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Without --locale, the image now gets the locale the build machine itself
    uses, and only that one. A German build host therefore produces a German
    image, default and all, with nothing to set in lts.conf - and the build
    says which locale it took.

    It used to copy the server's entire /etc/locale.gen, so a host with five
    locales ticked produced an image carrying five that nobody had asked
    for, and the one the image actually used was whatever the server's
    /etc/default/locale happened to say.

  * That file is no longer copied either, and this is the part worth
    knowing. update-locale routinely writes

        LANG=en_US.UTF-8
        LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8

    and LC_ALL overrides everything, lts.conf included. An image built from
    such a host could not be switched to another language by any means, and
    nothing said why. What is written into the image now is LANG and
    nothing else.

  * If the build machine has no real locale of its own - only C.UTF-8, as a
    minimal server often does - the build says so plainly rather than
    leaving it to be found at a client:

        NOTE: this machine has no locale of its own, so the image gets
        none. Every client will speak English whatever lts.conf says;
        pass --locale to change that.

  * ltsp-build-client(8) and lts.conf(5) follow: the image's language comes
    from the build machine unless --locale says otherwise, and LANG in
    lts.conf is for choosing among what --locale put there.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Sun, 20 Sep 2026 09:37:28 +0200

ltsp (6.3.14-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ltsp-build-client --locale takes a comma separated list, so an image can
    carry several locales and lts.conf can then choose between them per
    client with LANG:

        --locale de_DE.UTF-8,en_US.UTF-8

    The first is still the image's default, and a single value behaves
    exactly as before. Each entry may name its encoding after a space, as
    before.

    This is what makes the greeter's language an lts.conf setting rather
    than a property of the image. glibc refuses to translate anything at
    all in the C locale, and C.UTF-8 counts as C for that - so an image
    built without this option carries no language but English, and no
    setting in lts.conf can change it. Measured, not assumed: with
    en_US.UTF-8 generated, LANGUAGE=de gives a German greeter; with only
    C.UTF-8, neither LANG nor LANGUAGE does anything at all.

  * lts.conf(5): the language section rewritten around that. LANG in
    lts.conf switches the greeter per client; LANGUAGE does too and takes a
    bare language name, but needs a real locale in force first; and since
    ldm 3.4.4-1 the greeter logs the case where the locale is missing
    instead of coming up in English without a word.

  * ltsp-build-client(8): --locale documented at all, which it was not.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Sun, 20 Sep 2026 08:44:26 +0200

ltsp (6.3.13-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * lts.conf(5): the icons a theme may carry, which were nowhere listed -
    preferences, host, session, language, reboot, shutdown and backend, 24x24
    with an alpha channel, each falling back to the ltsp theme like every
    other theme file.

    With the reason they had not been visible: until ldm 3.4.3-1 none of them
    were drawn, whatever the theme held. The greeter had always asked for
    them and under GTK2 they appeared, but GTK 3.10 turned gtk-button-images
    and gtk-menu-images off and the request was ignored without a word. No
    lts.conf setting could have brought them back - it needed the GTK
    setting, which ldm 3.4.3-1 sets.

