ltsp (6.3.8-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS_n is no longer dropped when the same key is already in
    the image under another name. The duplicate check compared key type and
    key material and ignored the host, so two names for one key counted as a
    repeat.

    That is not a corner case, it is what an image built by
    ltsp-build-client hands you: the build bakes the server's own keys in
    under the name "server", while RDP_SERVER is normally an address. The
    address entry from lts.conf looked like a repeat and was discarded, so
    ssh-keygen -F found nothing for that address, and RDP_PREAUTH=ssh
    disabled itself: no ssh connection at all, straight through to the RDP
    client, one line in the client's log as the only trace.

    The check now includes the host. Covered by a harness that drives the
    real hook against a temporary key file: same host and key still does not
    grow the file, a second name for the same key is added, a malformed line
    and an unsupported key type are still refused. The old code fails that
    third case and passes the rest.

    lts.conf(5) says outright that the build leaves the server's keys under
    the name "server", that the address entry is still needed, and that
    RDP_PREAUTH_HOST=server is the way to get the same result on an image
    built before this fix.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 18:01:54 +0200

ltsp (6.3.7-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The client loads its console font before systemd starts, which removes
    the row of empty boxes from the early boot messages.

    The kernel's built-in font maps Latin-1 and nothing else. systemd writes
    its messages in the client's locale, quotes unit names typographically
    and ends them with an ellipsis - none of which exist in that font, so
    each of those characters is drawn as an empty box. console-setup fixes
    it, but its service is WantedBy=multi-user.target and runs long after
    the messages anyone reads have gone past.

    init-ltsp.d/50-console-font runs the font script console-setup already
    cached in the image, while init-ltsp still owns the machine, and puts
    the console into UTF-8 mode. No configuration to repeat and nothing new
    to install: the image was carrying the answer, just not early enough.

    CONSOLE_FONT in lts.conf names a different font for that early phase,
    documented in lts.conf(5) along with what it does not do - console-setup
    still loads its own font afterwards.

    Reported as "kleine Kloetzchen" where a special character belongs, seen
    on a German client whose image locale is de_DE.UTF-8. Not specific to
    German: an English one quotes with U+2018 and U+2019 and fares no better.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 17:51:03 +0200

ltsp (6.3.6-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * HOWTO: --base does not isolate the TFTP tree. It isolates the chroot and
    the image; 035-update-kernels still copies the new kernel and initrd
    into every TFTP root it knows about, and removes the ones that no longer
    correspond to a chroot. On a server with clients on it, that means they
    boot a different initrd than they did an hour ago, and an older kernel
    someone kept around is gone.

    Not a bug - that step exists so a freshly built chroot becomes bootable
    - but worth knowing before building "just to try" on a live server. The
    two log lines that say it are easy to read past, so they are quoted, and
    the entry says what to check afterwards: that the initrd the clients
    fetch still comes from the same chroot as the image they mount.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 17:38:11 +0200

ltsp (6.3.5-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ltsp-build-client re-reads the package lists after installing the early
    packages. Without that, an https --extra-mirror could not work at all on
    a first build: 020-apt-get-update runs against a minbase chroot that has
    no root certificates yet, so the mirror answers with

        SSL connection failed: certificate verify failed

    and apt continues with the sources it can reach. ca-certificates is
    installed one step later, as an early package - that is what the option
    is for - but nothing refreshed the lists afterwards, so 030-late-packages
    looked its packages up in an index that had never been fetched and the
    build stopped with

        E: Unable to locate package ldm

    The message named a package; the problem was an unreachable archive, and
    the two are several hundred log lines apart.

    Found building a client on a server whose only source for ldm and
    ltsp-client-core was the project's own signed archive, which is served
    over https and therefore hit this on every attempt.

    The plugin also returns early when no early packages were asked for,
    rather than running apt-get install with an empty list.

  * HOWTO: the same trap under "Traps, and how each one announces itself",
    with what it looks like in the build log.

