ltsp (6.3.4-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * lts.conf(5) now documents every directive the client actually reads,
    instead of only the ones this fork added. The page went from about
    thirty directives to a hundred and fifty, and it grew that way because
    the list was derived from the code rather than remembered: each name the
    client reads from its environment without setting it first, cross-checked
    against the shipped example files.

    New sections for sound, local devices, filesystems and home directories,
    swap, time, network and names, running commands at boot, local kernel
    updates, and the configuration file's own handling. The existing sections
    on the session backend, screens, keyboard, graphics and services gained
    the directives they had been missing - among them every LDM_ option, the
    whole XRANDR_ family, the X server and touchscreen settings, and the
    service-removal lists.

    Three things the audit turned up on the way. The page had two sections
    both called SCREENS, saying overlapping things; they are now one. The
    kiosk screen's account is called KIOSKUSER, without the underscore that
    would be natural - written down now, since guessing it costs an evening.
    And the boot-menu directives that look like lts.conf settings
    (BOOT_METHODS, the CMDLINE_ series, PXELINUX_DEFAULT) are not: they come
    from /etc/ltsp/update-kernels.conf, which the page now says outright.

    What is deliberately not listed: script-internal variables, the numbered
    instances of a documented series, and the settings that belong to
    ltsp-update-kernels(8).

