ltsp (6.3.3-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ltsp-client-core depends on iproute2, which it has always needed and
    never asked for. A client image built without it comes up with no
    /sbin/ip at all, and six scripts then do nothing - none of them
    noticeably, because a missing command in a pipeline yields an empty
    string rather than an error:

      - init-ltsp.d/09-hostname loses the part that tells clients apart, so
        every machine in the room is called "ltsp". The greeter shows that
        name, and it is the first thing anyone reads off a screen when
        reporting a fault.
      - init-ltsp.d/50-machine-id writes the same D-Bus machine id on every
        client, since the MAC it is built from comes out empty.
      - init-ltsp.d/50-valid-lft cannot re-arm an address whose DHCP lease
        expires, which is the thing that script exists for.
      - ltsp_config.d/50-client-mac leaves LTSP_CLIENT_MAC empty.
      - init-ltsp.d/10-resolv-conf cannot find the gateway to test as a
        fallback nameserver.
      - init-ltsp.d/02-initramfs-env cannot derive $DEVICE when the
        initramfs did not leave one.

    Found on a client whose greeter read "ltsp" with nothing after it.

  * lts.conf(5) documents HOSTNAME, HOSTNAME_BASE and HOSTNAME_EXTRA - the
    knobs that decide what a client calls itself - and LDM_CLOCK_FORMAT.
    None of them were written down anywhere.

