ltsp (6.3.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Shutting a client down from the greeter now actually switches the
    machine off, whether or not the image was booted with toram. Until now
    it was a race the client could lose: init-ltsp.d/50-systemd-shutdown
    replaces systemd-shutdown with a script that disconnects nbd - the old
    workaround for NetworkBlockDevice/nbd#51 - and only then runs
    "poweroff -f". Past that disconnect there is no root filesystem left:
    the squashfs underneath answers every read with EIO. And /sbin/poweroff
    under systemd is a symlink to systemctl, a large dynamically linked
    program that still has pages to fault in. When it lost the race the
    process died, PID 1 with it, and the client sat there with a black
    screen and the fans running - services visibly stopped, machine still
    on.

    The last step is now a write to /proc/sysrq-trigger instead. echo and
    redirection are shell builtins, /proc is not the root filesystem, and
    the kernel's sysrq handler calls kernel_power_off() itself rather than
    running a helper, so nothing has to be read from a disk that is already
    gone. Measured on a client booted without toram, the kernel reports
    "sysrq: Power Off" 1.5 ms after the nbd disconnect, ahead of the first
    squashfs read error; the old path needed the whole of systemctl to
    survive that window.

    "poweroff -f" stays as a fallback for a kernel built without
    CONFIG_MAGIC_SYSRQ, reached after a wait spun out of builtins so that
    even waiting costs no disk access. Verified on a thin client in all
    four combinations: toram and no toram, poweroff and reboot.

 -- Marc-Andre Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 14:56:04 +0200

ltsp (6.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Writing SCREEN_07=ldm in lts.conf no longer hangs the client. That line
    is the most natural thing an admin can write, and it was the cause:
    ltsp_config.d/15default-screen sets that default itself when no screen is
    configured at all, and sets a companion variable along with it -
    SCREEN_07_WAIT_FGCONSOLE=False. Setting SCREEN_07 by hand made the whole
    block be skipped, so the companion was never set, and screen_session then
    waited in

        while boolean_is_true "${WAIT_FGCONSOLE:-True}" &&
              [ $(fgconsole) -ne "$SCREEN_NUM" ]; do sleep 2; done

    for tty7 to become the foreground console. Under systemd nothing switches
    to it beforehand, and Xorg - which does switch, with its vt7 argument -
    only starts after that loop. It waited forever.

    The flag is now filled in for any SCREEN_nn set to ldm, whoever wrote it,
    and only when the admin has not set it explicitly, so forcing it to True
    stays possible for a setup that switches terminals by hand.

    What made this expensive to find is how healthy it looks from outside:
    ldm.service reads "active (running)", no unit has failed,
    graphical.target is reached, and there is neither an Xorg log nor an ldm
    log, because neither ever started. The only tell is screen_session with a
    sleep 2 child - and chvt 7 on the client confirms it in one command.

    Found on a second site running this image, where the client showed a
    console login instead of the greeter. Tested against five cases: the flag
    filled in for a hand-written SCREEN_07, left alone when set explicitly,
    applied per screen for several ldm screens, not applied to non-ldm
    screens, and matched when the value carries arguments.

  * HOWTO and lts.conf(5) document the trap, including what it looks like
    from the outside and the one-command confirmation.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 14:00:00 +0200

ltsp (6.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New: client/Debian/share/ltsp/ltsp-client-vendor-functions, overriding
    detect_arch() the same way the server side has always done. The server
    has had vendor overrides since forever; the client never did, so it fell
    back to the common implementation, `uname -m`, and called this
    architecture "x86_64" while the server calls it "amd64" and names the
    chroot and the TFTP tree accordingly.

    The disagreement is invisible with PXELINUX, because DHCP option 67
    carries a full path there (/ltsp/amd64/pxelinux.0) and
    init-ltsp.d/05-getltsconffile takes the directory from it, never reaching
    the fallback. With iPXE the boot file is just "ltsp.ipxe" with no
    directory at all, so the fallback is exactly what runs - and the client
    asks for /ltsp/x86_64/lts.conf, which does not exist. 05-getltsconffile
    only checks that what it fetched is non-empty, so the failure is silent:
    the client keeps whatever lts.conf was baked into the image and every
    change made on the server is ignored.

    Found in a log line that should have been looked for a week earlier:
        file /srv/tftp/ltsp/x86_64/lts.conf not found for 172.17.249.182

    This corrects 6.2.2-1, which claimed the TFTP fetch was verified. It was
    not: the settings the check found had come from a copy baked into the
    image in the same session.

  * HOWTO: BOOT_IMAGE= on the iPXE kernel line, which fixes the same problem
    from the other end and is needed anyway to give two images two different
    lts.conf files. 05-getltsconffile prefers the directory from BOOT_IMAGE
    over everything else.

  * HOWTO, new step 5c: a fat client image alongside the thin one. How the
    login actually works (ldm's ssh backend is the authentication; the same
    connection carries the identity, so no client joins the domain), how to
    get /home over NFS instead of sshfs, and four traps that each cost an
    afternoon:

    - Two chroots both named "amd64" overwrite each other in the TFTP tree,
      which is keyed on the chroot name and not on the base directory. The
      fat build silently replaced the thin client's initrd. Hence
      <arch>-<role> naming.
    - A desktop metapackage brings its own display manager: xfce4 pulls in
      lightdm, whose postinst takes display-manager.service away from ldm.
      The client then stops at a text login with no error anywhere.
    - With several matching dhcp-boot lines dnsmasq uses the one it read
      last, not the most specific one, so a per-MAC exception has to sort
      after the general rule in /etc/dnsmasq.d.
    - lts.conf cannot choose which image is loaded - by the time anything
      reads it, the image is running. LTSP_FATCLIENT only decides what an
      image that has a desktop does with it.

  * lts.conf(5): where SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS_n lines come from (ssh-keyscan -t
    ed25519, or the server's own ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub with the address
    prepended and the comment dropped), and what RDP_PREAUTH=ssh does when
    there is no key for the host it would ask - see ldm 3.3.0.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Thu, 17 Sep 2026 13:00:00 +0200

ltsp (6.2.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * HOWTO: lts.conf over TFTP needs a tftp client in the image, and nothing
    says so when it is missing. init-ltsp.d/05-getltsconffile fetches it with
    the plain "tftp" command, which ltsp-build-client does not install, and
    the script only checks that what it fetched is non-empty - so with no
    tftp binary the client keeps the stub that 030-default-lts-conf wrote
    during finalization, silently.

    The symptom is a greeter that comes up perfectly and is configured for
    nothing: no RDP_SERVER, the ssh backend by default, a US keyboard, and
    KEEP_SYSTEM_SERVICES ignored so sshd is gone as well. It reads as
    "lts.conf is being ignored", which is true, two steps earlier than it
    appears. tftp-hpa is now in the documented --late-packages list, with
    the trap written up next to the others.

    Verified on a PXE-booted client afterwards: the whole chain from
    ipxe.efi through ltsp.ipxe, kernel, initrd and NBD to a running greeter,
    with lts.conf fetched over TFTP and its settings present in the greeter
    process environment.

  * HOWTO: UEFI clients get no boot offer when dnsmasq only has pxe-service.
    That is the PXE boot menu protocol, which most UEFI firmware does not
    answer - it reports PXE-E16 "No valid offer received" even though the
    address was assigned. They need the boot file named directly in the DHCP
    reply, tied to the client architecture (RFC 4578: 7 and 9 for EFI
    x86-64). Having both configured is worse than having only dhcp-boot:
    dnsmasq then points the firmware at the boot server protocol it will not
    speak.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:45:00 +0200

ltsp (6.2.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * lts.conf(5): document the lockout warnings that ldm 3.2.0 adds -
    RDP_PREAUTH_MAXFAIL, RDP_PREAUTH_FAILWINDOW, RDP_PREAUTH_LOCKTIME -
    together with the reason they hang off the pre-authentication rather
    than off RDP itself. Measured against xrdp: a rejected password makes
    the RDP client neither exit nor report anything, so failed RDP logins
    are not observable from the client at all, and the pre-auth is the one
    place a rejection can be seen. Notes that the tally is shared by every
    screen on the client, because the thing doing the banning counts per
    address and four greeters share one.

  * HOWTO: two traps found while building the image the documented way.

    ltsp-build-client --apt-keys needs gpg on the build host, not in the
    image: Debian/001-apt-keys runs "gpg --dearmor" as a plain command,
    and a minimal Debian ships only gpgv because that is all apt needs.
    The build unpacks the whole base system first and then stops with
    "gpg: command not found". curl is missing for the same reason - the
    archive instructions assume it - so both are now listed under the
    prerequisites.

    ltsp-update-image does not restart nbd-server, and says so in a line
    that is easy to read past. Until it is restarted, nbd-server keeps
    serving the image it already had open, so a rebuilt image changes
    nothing visible. Also noted that --cleanup is a flag: passing it a
    value makes that value the chroot name.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 10:30:00 +0200

ltsp (6.2.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * New: SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS and SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS_n in lts.conf, added to
    /etc/ssh/ssh_known_hosts at boot by init-ltsp.d/06-ssh-known-hosts.
    RDP_PREAUTH=ssh refuses to proceed against a host key it does not
    recognise, and a thin client keeps no known_hosts between boots - so
    the keys have to be known in advance. Until now that meant baking them
    into the image with ltsp-update-sshkeys(8) and rebuilding 650 MB to add
    a server or rotate a key. Naming them in lts.conf also allows per-client
    sections, so a pilot group can trust a different set of servers than
    production does, which a flat file in the image cannot express.
  * Entries are appended, so image-baked keys survive; a line already
    present is not added twice; a malformed line or unrecognised key type is
    refused with a message rather than written, because ssh stops reading
    known_hosts at the first line it cannot parse and would discard
    everything below it. Numbering stops at the first gap so a mistyped
    index costs one key rather than all the later ones.
  * lts.conf(5) gains that, a section on the per-screen SCREEN_nn_ prefix -
    which has always worked and was never written down - and a FILES note
    that lts.conf is fetched over TFTP at boot and overwrites the copy in
    the image, which is what makes the above possible and also what limits
    how much it is worth.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Sat, 12 Sep 2026 00:30:00 +0200

ltsp (6.1.0-2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Fixed: the scripts under /usr/share/ltsp shipped without their
    executable bit, so a client booted to a text login and systemd reported
    ldm.service as "status=203/EXEC" - which names no file and reads like a
    missing binary rather than a wrong mode. Found on a PXE-booted client:
    /usr/share/ltsp/screen_session was -rw-r--r--.
  * Cause was the build, not the tree: git records screen_session, xinitrc
    and twelve others as 100755, but a Windows checkout has core.filemode
    false and carries no executable bit at all, and debian/rules copies that
    tree with cp -a. Only the handful of files installed explicitly with
    install -m0755 survived. The build now restores the modes git records
    before packaging.
  * 6.1.0-1 and 6.0.0-5 are affected and should not be used.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 23:15:00 +0200

ltsp (6.1.0-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The provisioning tools under server/tools are now installed into
    /usr/sbin, each with a manual page in section 8: build-apt-repo,
    build-autoinstall-iso, provision-samba-ad-dc, setup-home-server,
    setup-lb-addressing, setup-ltsp-app and setup-ltsp-root. They existed
    only in the git tree until now, so anyone installing ltsp-server from
    the archive got the documentation's subject but not its object.
  * The manual pages carry the findings, not just the syntax: why the
    domain join uses adcli rather than realmd, why sssd needs
    ad_gpo_map_remote_interactive before a correct password is accepted,
    why an orphaned xfce4-session makes every later login fail silently,
    why the offload unit is useless without ethtool installed, and why a
    keepalived pair on DHCP takes itself apart.
  * HOWTO-lab-from-scratch and PXE-network-boot-test are installed into
    /usr/share/doc/ltsp-server; they were written but never shipped.
  * The preseeds are installed into /usr/share/ltsp-server/preseed as
    reference material for build-autoinstall-iso(8).
  * RDP_PREAUTH=True now selects the method rather than meaning Kerberos
    (ldm >= 3.0.3-1): a configured realm picks Kerberos, no realm picks the
    ssh probe, and False still means no check at all. Documented with the
    one asymmetry that matters - the automatic fallback to ssh runs only
    where the host keys are present, because the probe does not fail open
    on an unverified key and an upgrade must not turn a working login into
    a locked one.
  * lts.conf(5) documents RDP_PREAUTH=ssh, RDP_PREAUTH_HOST and
    RDP_PREAUTH_SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS (ldm >= 3.0.3-1): the credential check for
    sites with local accounts and no KDC. It records what that variant
    costs as well as what it buys - it sends the password to the host it
    asks, where Kerberos does not, so the host keys belong in the image
    via ltsp-update-sshkeys(8), which copies ssh_host_*_key.pub and
    nothing secret. RDP_PREAUTH_HOST exists because HAProxy forwards 3389
    only: ssh to the load balancer address would reach the balancer, not a
    session server.
  * ltsp-swapfile-delete(8) is documented too - it was the last command in
    the package without a manual page. Writing it turned up a real fault
    worth recording: the script ends by calling /usr/sbin/tmpwatch, a Red
    Hat tool that Debian does not package at all (the equivalent being
    tmpreaper), and then exits 0 regardless. On Debian it marks the live
    clients and deletes nothing, while cron records a success - so the swap
    directory only ever grows and no error explains it. The BUGS section
    gives the tmpreaper invocation to replace it with.
  * New: lts.conf(5). The example file has always pointed at "your installed
    lts.conf man page" and this fork never shipped one. It documents the
    directives added or changed here - LDM_DEFAULT_BACKEND and
    LDM_FORCE_BACKEND with their precedence, RDP_SERVER, RDP_OPTIONS,
    RDP_PREAUTH and RDP_PREAUTH_REALM, XKBLAYOUT with CONSOLE_KEYMAP,
    SCREEN_nn against ldm.service and ltsp-screen@, X_COLOR_DEPTH,
    LTSP_FATCLIENT, KEEP_SYSTEM_SERVICES and LDM_NBD_CHECKUPDATE - and
    points at the example file for the upstream remainder.
  * server/configs/lts.conf documents the directives added since 5.18:
    LDM_DEFAULT_BACKEND, RDP_SERVER, RDP_OPTIONS, RDP_PREAUTH,
    RDP_PREAUTH_REALM, LDM_NBD_CHECKUPDATE and KEEP_SYSTEM_SERVICES, plus
    CONSOLE_KEYMAP next to XKBLAYOUT - a layout mismatch mistypes the
    password and reads as a rejected login - and a note on SCREEN_07
    against ldm.service and ltsp-screen@.

  * Numbered 6.1.0 rather than a fifth revision of 6.0.0: a Debian revision
    says the packaging changed, and this changes what the package provides -
    seven commands in /usr/sbin that were not there before, nine manual
    pages, and two documents that existed only in the source tree.

 -- Marc-Andre Beckmann-Butz <ma@butz.online>  Fri, 11 Sep 2026 21:30:00 +0200

ltsp (6.0.0-4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * ltsp-screen@.service is shipped but no longer enabled on tty1. The
    greeter is started by ldm.service (ldm >= 3.0.2-1), which runs
    screen_session 07 for the SCREEN_07=ldm default that
    ltsp_config.d/15default-screen already sets; enabling an instance here
    as well put a second greeter on tty1 and had the two fighting over
    which VT owns the session. The template stays for the cases it is
    actually useful for - a second screen on another VT, or a screen
    script other than ldm - and is enabled by hand.

